ilable. Stronger or more persistent coordination can slow release of reactive metal species, while ligand exchange can open alternative conversion pathways.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr400544s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr400544s","kind":"review","title":"Mechanisms of Nucleation and Growth of Nanoparticles in Solution","container":"Chemical Reviews","year":2014}},{"id":"nonmetal-source","title":"Conversion of a nonmetal precursor","summary":"Chalcogen and pnictogen precursors can differ in how they release or transfer the element during a reaction. Bonding, coordination and the reaction medium can influence the activation or transfer steps that make an element available for growth.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr900137k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr900137k","kind":"review","title":"Prospects of Colloidal Nanocrystals for Electronic and Optoelectronic Applications","container":"Chemical Reviews","year":2009}}]}
